import hashlib
v=open('v170.txt',encoding='utf-8').read()
E='''  - A STRONG'S NUMBER NAMED FOR A VERSE THAT DOES NOT HOLD IT, AND A STATED GREEK VOICE THAT THE STORED FORM DOES NOT BEAR, ARE RECORDED (pkgSPC rev 2, ASK 279; L2 Count Plan computes, Build Save Body writes; TRIAL, record-only):
    - Why: in run 37032 a challenger [L2] row named harah (H2029) as standing in Isaiah 7:14. original_words holds ha.Rah H2030 (an adjective) there; H2029 stands in Isaiah 8:3. The [L2] check counted the word and did not test the number against the verse.
    - What it does: in each [L2] tag, a Strong's number is paired with a verse only when a placing word stands just before the verse ("in", "at", "stands in", "word of/in/at", "verb of/in/at", "same <x> as", "shared with", "as in"), or when the verse is chained to such a verse by "and" or a comma (not ", and"). No pairing is made after "after", "before", "through", "than", "not", "question of", "gives", "defines", "joins" or "sign of"; a range is not paired; an H number pairs only with an Old Testament verse and a G number only with a New Testament verse; no flag is written when the verse holds another number named in the same sentence. When the paired verse does not hold the number, the tag carries "<number> is named as standing in <verse>, which does not hold it", with the number that stands there for the row's nearest word when one is found, and the verses that do hold it. When a row states one voice (active, passive or middle) for one Greek word in one verse and none of the stored morph codes bears it, the tag carries "<word> (<number>) is called <voice>; in <verse> its form is <morph>". Build Save Body writes one line: "[AUDIT v52: [L2] PLACE AND FORM CHECK (pkgSPC, ASK 279; TRIAL, record-only) -- <round> <words>: <finding>; ... REVIEW THIS ROW.]", at most 12 tags. No count, grade or verdict changes.
    - Size: L2 Count Plan 24,648 to 27,805; Build Save Body 79,599 to 79,923.
    - Shown offline: over 9,537 tags of 60 stored debates, 6 flags, all read true against original_words (H2029 for Isaiah 7:14, three times; H7130 for 2 Samuel 7:12; G1097 for John 6:42; G4690 for Acts 2:30). Run 37032: 1 flag of 185 tags (the challenger round 4 harah row). Five planted cases behave as designed.
    - Limits: recall is traded for precision (a placing claim with no verse, such as "stands there", is not paired, and some earlier true flags are lost); a voice misstatement outside an [L2] row (the Luke 1:13 passive in an [L4] sentence of run 37032) is out of reach; the voice check reads Greek only, one voice word and one verse per row. Not shown by a run.
  - A BOOK-LESS "c:v" IN AN [L2] TAG'S OWN LINE BINDS TO THE ONE BOOK THAT LINE NAMES (pkgVBC rev 2, ASK 281; Citation Scan only):
    - Why: a tag line such as "[L2] ... in Luke 1:35 ... and 1:32" left "1:32" unbound, so the tag lost a verse it names.
    - What it does: a "c:v" with no book binds only when (i) it stands in the tag's own line; (ii) that line names exactly one book, counting full references and bare book names; (iii) a full reference of that book in chapter c stands before the "c:v" in the line; and (iv) it does not stand right after a capitalised word. There is no fallback to the block. The lexicon map still decides whether the word is counted.
    - Size: Citation Scan 30,066 to 31,291.
    - Shown offline: over 9,537 tags of 60 stored debates (eight rounds fed separately), 11 tags gain verses and none loses any; each was read and none binds a wrong book. Run 37032 round D3 [L4] line gains Luke 1:35 and Luke 1:32. A line naming two books binds nothing.
    - Limits: the effect of the 11 gained verses on COUNTED or NOT COUNTED is not measured; a "c:v" in a line that names no book, or two books, stays unbound. Not shown by a run.
  - THE ANSWER CHECK ROUND LABEL NAMES THE ROUND THAT HOLDS THE QUOTED ANSWER (ASK 277 part (a); Panel Build and Build Independent Verdict, the ANSWER CHECK line form):
    - Why: in run 37032 luna labelled a Luke 1:35 check [CR3] although its quoted ANSWER stands only in [DR3] (see pkgACL above). The form's bare "[<round>]" did not say which round the label names.
    - What it does: in the ANSWER CHECK line form the placeholder "[<round>]" becomes "[<the round that holds the quoted ANSWER; with ANSWER: NONE, the answering side's round for that press>]". Only the old placeholder is removed. The readers that take the label as the answer's round are unchanged.
    - Size: Panel Build 149,872 to 149,968; Build Independent Verdict 124,459 to 124,555.
    - Limits: a prompt wording change only; whether seats follow it is not shown by a run. pkgACL (above) records a label that still names the other side's round.
  - AN ORDER OF EVENTS STATED WITH NO VERSE IN THE SENTENCE IS LISTED FOR THE READER (pkgORD rev 2, ASK 278; Build Save Body only; TRIAL, record-only; no seat sees it):
    - Why: in run 37032 the defender's round 1 stated "The birth came first, and the marriage came after." Matthew 1:24 ("took unto him his wife") and 1:25 ("till she had brought forth") set the taking before the birth. No check reads a statement of fact for its order: the closing contradiction enforcer reads only the Oracle's contradictions block, declared reclaims and denials of a narrowing.
    - What it does: in each of the eight rounds, a sentence is listed when it states an explicit two-step order ("X came first, and Y came after", "first ..., then ...", "<verb> ... first and (then) ... after/second/follows", "first to X and then to Y") and has no verse reference and no quotation. A sentence in the first person (I, my, me, any case) is skipped. Each listed sentence carries the references cited in its paragraph. One line per debate, at most 10 sentences: "[AUDIT v53: ORDER STATEMENT LIST (pkgORD, ASK 278; TRIAL, record-only; no seat sees it) -- N sentence(s) state an order of events with no verse in the sentence; check each against the KJV rows: (1) [<round>] "..." (rows cited in its paragraph: ...) ... REVIEW THIS ROW.]". The line lists and never judges; no count, grade or verdict changes.
    - Size: Build Save Body 79,923 to 81,554 (live, after pkgSPC).
    - Shown offline: over 61 stored debates (60 plus run 37032), 12 sentences in 11 debates. Against the rows, the known-bad 37032 sentence is listed, the known-good "the knowing comes first and the conceiving after" (Genesis 4:1) is listed, and one listed sentence states no order of events.
    - Limits: the rows listed are those cited in the sentence's paragraph, so the row that settles the order may stand elsewhere; an order stated with a verse in the sentence, or in another form ("X preceded Y"), is not listed; a reader of a sentence on Matthew 1:18 keeps "came together" (under one roof), "took" (1:24) and "knew" (1:25) apart. Not shown by a run.
'''
anchor='\n\n## 10. The panel\n'; assert v.count(anchor)==1
v2=v.replace(anchor,'\n'+E.rstrip('\n')+anchor)
old='- Live flow: s201 (n8n version c2501cd3, 455 nodes; Shared Library 931,428 characters, sha a894ff926b24be72).'
assert v2.count(old)==1
v2=v2.replace(old,'- Live flow: s205 (n8n version 7d290a06, 455 nodes; Shared Library 931,428 characters, sha a894ff926b24be72).')
old2=' + pkgACL (s201).'; assert v2.count(old2)==1
v2=v2.replace(old2,' + pkgACL (s201). s205 = s201 + pkgSPC rev 2 (s202) + pkgVBC rev 2 (s203) + ASK 277 part (a) (s204) + pkgORD rev 2 (s205).')
old3='`ANSWER CHECK: <ref> -- [<round>] -- MEANS:'; assert v2.count(old3)==1
v2=v2.replace(old3,'`ANSWER CHECK: <ref> -- [<the round that holds the quoted ANSWER; with ANSWER: NONE, the answering side\'s round for that press>] -- MEANS:')
old4='an answer found in no round, shorter than 5 words, or not quoted is skipped with no record;'; assert v2.count(old4)==1
v2=v2.replace(old4,'an answer found in no round, shorter than 5 words, or not quoted, and a line with no [CRn] or [DRn] label, are skipped with no record;')
open('v170r5.txt','w',encoding='utf-8').write(v2)
print(len(v),len(v2),len(v2)-len(v),hashlib.md5(v2.encode()).hexdigest(), v2.count('—'))
